# user/bootlogd.ad — KEEP THE BOOT LOG ON THE STICK.
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS EXISTS FOR, AND IT IS ABOUT THE HUMAN LOOP RATHER THAN THE
# CODE.
#
# The owner boots a Lenovo from a USB stick this tree builds. He has no serial
# cable, no shell and no second machine attached. When something goes wrong the
# ONLY evidence that survives is a PHOTOGRAPH OF THE SCREEN -- and a photograph
# shows the last forty lines, in whatever state the console left them, with the
# compositor very likely painted over the top of them.
#
# So: the stick keeps its own log. After a bad boot he powers the machine off
# with the button, plugs the stick into another computer, and opens a file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE IT IS WRITTEN, AND WHY THAT FILESYSTEM
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ESP. FAT32. `\HAMNIX.LOG` in the root of the boot partition.
#
# Not the ext4 root, and the reasoning is not the obvious one:
#
#   1. HE HAS TO BE ABLE TO OPEN IT. FAT32 mounts on Windows, on macOS and on
#      Linux with nothing installed. ext4 is fine on his Debian host and is not
#      fine on whatever machine he is standing next to. A log he cannot read is
#      not evidence.
#
#   2. THE JOURNAL ARGUMENT RUNS THE OTHER WAY. "ext4 has a journal and FAT
#      does not" sounds like it settles this, and it settles it backwards. A
#      journal keeps a filesystem CONSISTENT after a crash; it does not keep
#      YOUR DATA. tests/linux/install_from_usb.sh has the measurement, in its
#      own words: SIGKILL to QEMU is a power cut, ext4 commits on a 5 s timer,
#      "the write was still in the guest's page cache" and the next boot
#      behaved as though the root were not persistent. The journal did its job
#      perfectly and the data was gone anyway. So ext4's journal buys this file
#      nothing, and its cost -- a journal transaction per synchronous write --
#      is real.
#
#   3. WHAT ACTUALLY MAKES IT SURVIVE THE POWER BUTTON IS A DISCIPLINE, NOT A
#      FILESYSTEM, and the discipline is what makes FAT's missing journal
#      irrelevant:
#
#        * The file is PREALLOCATED TO ITS FULL SIZE AT IMAGE BUILD TIME
#          (scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh) and is NEVER EXTENDED. Every write is an
#          overwrite in place. No cluster is ever allocated, the FAT chain is
#          never mutated, the directory entry's length and start cluster never
#          change. The only thing that has to reach the medium is the data
#          blocks -- which is exactly the part a journal would not have
#          protected either.
#
#        * Every write is O_SYNC (user/linux-syscalls.c:sys_open_sync), so
#          write(2) does not return until those blocks are on the stick. The
#          window in which a power cut loses anything is one snapshot interval,
#          not one journal commit.
#
#      The same two properties are why this CANNOT FILL THE FILESYSTEM: there
#      is no code path anywhere that makes the file bigger than it was built,
#      and the size is a constant in the build script.
#
#      This is also, independently, the arrangement Hamnix's own bare-metal
#      line arrived at -- kernel/printk/esp_log.ad snapshots the printk ring
#      into a preallocated extent on the ESP. Two lines of this project reached
#      the same design from opposite ends, which is the best evidence available
#      that it is the right one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT CAPTURES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE KERNEL'S HALF AND THE SHELL'S HALF, IN ONE ORDERED, TIMESTAMPED STREAM.
#
# The kernel's half was never the problem: it is in the printk ring, and dmesg
# shows it. The interesting half -- the half that was invisible on 2026-08-15,
# when the laptop reached `exec /bin/hamsh /etc/rc.boot` and the screen stopped
# changing -- is what rc.boot and the desktop say. That is now in the ring too:
# user/linux-syscalls.c:consmirror copies every console write, at the one choke
# point every Adder program's output passes through, into /dev/kmsg at <7>.
# <7> is recorded and NOT printed at loglevel=7, so it costs nothing on screen;
# the long note there is the argument.
#
# So this program does not have to capture anything itself. It is a SNAPSHOTTER
# of a buffer the kernel already keeps for us, and that is what makes it cheap,
# what makes it correct across many writing processes (the ring is one
# kernel-serialised buffer; a file opened by every process would be a race of
# independent write offsets), and what makes STARTING IT LATE COST NOTHING --
# the ring already holds the whole boot, so the first snapshot contains t=0.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT CAPTURE, said here so nobody has to discover it:
#   * output from a process that has DROPPED PRIVILEGE. /dev/kmsg is writable
#     by root; the LIVE image's `setuid 1001` and etc/rc.de-user's desktop
#     users cannot open it. The INSTALLED boot -- etc/rc.boot.installed, the
#     one on the owner's stick -- never drops privilege, so its rc, services
#     and desktop are all captured.
#   * anything a program writes to a REDIRECT, a pipe or a terminal window.
#     Only writes to the console device itself (rdev 5:1) are mirrored, which
#     is deliberate: the alternative pours every `cat` into the kernel ring.
#   * what the SCREEN looked like. A log records text, not pixels.
#
# ===========================================================================
# IT MUST NEVER BE A WAY FOR THE MACHINE TO STOP WORKING
# ===========================================================================
# THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS PROGRAM WAS A STANDING LOAD ON THE BOOT MEDIUM,
# AND ALMOST ALL OF IT WAS REDUNDANT. That is not a suspicion; it is measured,
# by tests/linux/wedge_hunt.sh, on the shipped medium booted as usb-storage:
#
#     AN IDLE HAMNIX DESKTOP WROTE 45.9 MB TO ITS OWN BOOT MEDIUM IN ROUGHLY
#     100 SECONDS -- about 460 KB/s, in 1096 write operations and 381 CACHE
#     FLUSHES -- AND IT HAD NOTHING NEW TO SAY.
#
# Every one of those flushes came from here. The old snapshot() rewound to
# offset 0 and wrote the header, the whole of the kernel ring and a terminator
# EVERY TWO SECONDS, through an O_SYNC descriptor, WHETHER OR NOT ONE BYTE OF
# THE RING HAD CHANGED. Seven write(2) calls, so seven trips to the medium, so
# on a filesystem with no journal to batch them SEVEN SYNCHRONOUS FLUSHES every
# two seconds for as long as the machine is on.
#
# In a VM the ESP is a host file and that is nearly free, which is exactly why
# it survived review. ON A PHYSICAL USB STICK it is not free at all: the same
# 256 KiB of logical blocks is rewritten 150 times in five minutes, and a cheap
# controller's flash translation layer answers a rewrite of a region smaller
# than its erase block with a read-modify-write of the WHOLE erase block. The
# root filesystem is on the same device, so every exec, every page faulted back
# in and every config file opened by the desktop queues behind it.
#
# THE FIX IS NOT TO BE QUIETER. It is to STOP WRITING BYTES THAT ARE ALREADY
# THERE, which is a different thing and costs the log nothing at all.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE FILE IS BUILT IN MEMORY AND ONLY THE PART THAT CHANGED IS WRITTEN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `img` holds exactly what this program wants the 256 KiB file to contain --
# header, ring, terminator, and newline fill to the end. `prev` holds what is
# already on the medium. A snapshot compares them, finds the first and last
# byte that differ, and issues ONE lseek and ONE write covering just that span.
#
# The consequences, in the order they matter:
#
#   * AN IDLE MACHINE WRITES NOTHING. No lseek, no write, no flush, no
#     transaction with the stick of any kind. If the ring has not changed there
#     is nothing to persist, and the old code's answer to that was to persist
#     it again anyway.
#
#   * A MACHINE THAT IS SAYING SOMETHING WRITES WHAT IT SAID. Until the ring
#     wraps, a new log line only appends: the differing span is the new tail
#     and the terminator, tens of bytes rather than sixty kilobytes. After the
#     ring wraps the whole buffer shifts and the span is the whole file again --
#     and that is correct, because at that point the whole file really is
#     different. §2 is what bounds THAT case.
#
#   * SEVEN FLUSHES BECOME ONE. The header and the terminator are part of the
#     same in-memory image as the ring, so they are not separate writes any
#     more. On flash the number of flushes matters at least as much as the
#     number of bytes.
#
#   * THE ONE-TIME WIPE DISAPPEARS, and it disappears by construction rather
#     than by being deleted. `prev` is BSS, so it starts as 256 KiB of NULs;
#     `img` is text and newline fill and contains no NUL anywhere; so on the
#     FIRST snapshot every byte differs and the span is the entire file. The
#     old wipe_file() -- 32 separate 8 KiB O_SYNC writes at startup -- was
#     doing that job in 32 flushes instead of one, and it existed only so that
#     a previous boot's tail could not be read as this boot's. It still cannot
#     be: the fill is part of the image.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. AND IT BACKS OFF WHEN THE MEDIUM IS SLOW, AND SAYS SO IN THE LOG
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# §1 removes the load that should never have existed. It does not bound the
# load on a machine that IS producing log lines fast, and it cannot know in
# advance how slow the stick in this particular laptop is -- that is precisely
# the thing that differs between the VM every gate runs in and the hardware the
# owner holds.
#
# So the write is TIMED, and the answer the medium gives decides the cadence.
# A snapshot whose write took longer than SLOW_MS doubles the interval, up to
# SNAP_MAX_MS; one comfortably under it halves the interval back down, to
# SNAP_MS and no further. The logger therefore cannot occupy more than a small
# and self-limiting share of a slow device, whatever the ring is doing.
#
# THE TRANSITION IS ANNOUNCED ON THE CONSOLE, which means consmirror puts it in
# the ring, which means the NEXT snapshot puts it in the log. So a stick that is
# too slow to log to says so IN ITS OWN LOG, in one line, and the person reading
# the file afterwards learns the fact that mattered most.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY *NOT* DONE, AND WHY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STOP AFTER THE BOOT COMPLETES -- NO. There is no such moment, and the failure
# this whole investigation is about happened FIVE MINUTES IN, on a desktop that
# had come up fine. A logger that stops when the boot "finishes" is a logger
# that is guaranteed to be off for every interesting failure. The forever loop
# is not an oversight; it is the requirement.
#
# REFUSE TO RUN ON REMOVABLE MEDIA AFTER N SNAPSHOTS -- NO. The medium is
# ALWAYS removable here; that is the whole premise. "After N snapshots" is just
# "turn the log off shortly after boot" with an extra step, and it fails in the
# same direction as stopping after the boot.
#
# DROP O_SYNC -- NO. It is the single property that makes the file worth
# trusting after the power button, and dropping it turns a slow log into a log
# that lies. What was wrong was never that the writes were synchronous; it was
# that there were writes at all when nothing had changed.
#
# JUST MAKE THE INTERVAL LONGER -- NO, and this is the interesting one. It
# trades the power-cut window -- the one thing the cadence buys -- for a
# reduction that §1 gets for free, and it would STILL have rewritten sixty
# kilobytes of identical bytes every time it fired. Lengthening the interval
# treats the symptom of a program that was writing the wrong thing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERY FAILURE IS STILL A MESSAGE AND AN EXIT 0
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It is spawned DETACHED from the rc, so nothing waits for it even when it
# works. If the ESP is missing, unmountable, or the log file is absent or
# unwritable, the boot continues and says why on the screen. A logging facility
# that can stop a boot is worse than no logging facility.
#
# AND IT IS NEVER SILENT ABOUT BEING UNWRITTEN. The preallocated file ships
# with text in it saying it has not been written by any boot yet. An empty file
# looks identical whether nothing was written or the write went somewhere else
# -- which is the exact bug this whole effort came out of -- so the unwritten
# state is made to SAY that it is the unwritten state.
#
# Usage: bootlogd [-1]        -1 = take one snapshot and exit (the gates)
# Adder notes: every buffer is a top-level BSS Array; no adjacent string
# literal concatenation.

extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_close(fd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_open_sync(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_klogread(buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_lseek(fd: int32, offset: int64, whence: int32) -> int64
extern def sys_errstr(buf: Ptr[uint8], nbuf: uint64) -> int32
extern def sys_waitfds(fds: Ptr[int32], nfds: uint64, timeout_ms: int64) -> int64
extern def sys_clock_gettime(clockid: int32, tp: Ptr[uint64]) -> int64
extern def sys_exit(code: uint64)

SEEK_SET: int32 = 0
CLOCK_MONOTONIC: int32 = 1

# THE FILE, AND ITS SIZE, WHICH IS THE SIZE POLICY.
#
# 256 KiB. It must match BOOTLOG_BYTES in scripts/hamlinux_disk.sh, which is
# what actually creates the file; if the two disagree the smaller one wins
# harmlessly, because this program never writes past what it was given and
# never extends anything.
#
# 256 KiB is chosen from what a boot produces: a loglevel=7 boot of this image
# is tens of kilobytes of kernel messages plus a few kilobytes of rc output.
# When the ring holds more than fits, sys_klogread hands back the TAIL -- the
# end of the boot, which is the part that matters after one went wrong.
#
# THE PATH IS WRITTEN OUT AT THE ONE CALL SITE rather than held in a top-level
# `BOOTLOG_PATH: Ptr[char] = "..."`, and that is not a style preference. Adder
# does not initialise a top-level pointer from a string literal: the variable
# is emitted, the program compiles and links with no diagnostic of any kind,
# and open(2) is handed a garbage pointer and fails EFAULT. MEASURED -- the
# first run of this program printed "could not be opened for writing: Bad
# address" while sitting in a directory where the honest answer was "No such
# file". Nothing else in user/*.ad or lib/*.ad uses that form.
BOOTLOG_CAP:  uint64 = 262144

# Room kept at the end of the image for the terminator and, on the failure
# path, the two lines that explain an unreadable ring. Generous on purpose:
# running the ring into the terminator would truncate the sentence a reader
# needs to know where this boot's text stops.
TAIL_RESERVE: uint64 = 512

# THE BASE CADENCE. THIS IS THE SIZE OF THE WINDOW IN WHICH A POWER CUT LOSES
# SOMETHING. Two seconds: short enough that the tail of a boot that died is on
# the stick, long enough that a whole boot costs on the order of a dozen
# writes -- and, now that a snapshot with nothing to say costs NO write at all,
# short enough to leave at that on any medium fast enough to keep up.
SNAP_MS: int64 = 2000

# ... AND THE CEILING THE BACKOFF MAY REACH. A minute is a long power-cut
# window and it is the right trade at the point where it is reached: it is only
# reached on a medium where each write is taking the better part of a second,
# and on such a medium the alternative to a long window is a machine so busy
# writing its log that the log is the only thing it does.
SNAP_MAX_MS: int64 = 60000

# The write time that means "this medium cannot keep up" and the one that means
# "it can again". Two different numbers, deliberately, so the cadence does not
# oscillate around a single threshold.
SLOW_MS: uint64 = 400
FAST_MS: uint64 = 100

# Never dereferenced at nfds == 0 -- the tree's portable park, as
# user/sleep.ad's header explains.
park_fds: Array[1, int32]

# WHAT THE FILE SHOULD CONTAIN, AND WHAT IT ALREADY CONTAINS.
#
# `prev` is BSS and therefore NUL, and `img` never contains a NUL -- it is
# header text, kernel log text, terminator text and newline fill. That is what
# makes the first snapshot a full-file write with no special case, and it is
# what replaced the old 32-write startup wipe.
img:    Array[262144, uint8]
prev:   Array[262144, uint8]
errbuf: Array[128, uint8]
numbuf: Array[24, uint8]
ts:     Array[2, uint64]

logfd:  int32 = -1
img_n:  uint64 = 0
interval: int64 = 2000
backed_off: int32 = 0


def cstrlen(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    n: uint64 = 0
    while s[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    return n


def wr(fd: int32, s: Ptr[uint8]):
    # LENGTH FROM THE STRING, NEVER FROM A NUMBER TYPED BESIDE IT. The first
    # version of this file passed a hand-counted byte count to each sys_write,
    # and several of them were short by one -- so the log came back with its
    # header lines run together, the trailing newline of each swallowed. It
    # still looked broadly right, which is the worst way for a diagnostic file
    # to be wrong. Nothing here counts bytes by hand any more.
    sys_write(fd, s, cstrlen(s))


def say(msg: Ptr[uint8]):
    # To the console, which is the screen on the shipped command line. These
    # lines are themselves mirrored into the ring by consmirror, so the log
    # explains its own state -- including, on the failure path, why there is
    # no log, and including the backoff notice, which is the one fact about a
    # slow stick that the file could otherwise never carry.
    wr(1, "bootlogd: ")
    wr(1, msg)
    wr(1, "\n")


def sayerr(msg: Ptr[uint8]):
    # WITH THE REASON THE KERNEL GAVE. "could not open the log" and "could not
    # open the log: Read-only file system" are different faults with different
    # fixes, and the person reading this is standing in front of a laptop with
    # no shell.
    n: int32 = sys_errstr(&errbuf[0], 128)
    wr(2, "bootlogd: ")
    wr(2, msg)
    if n > 0:
        wr(2, ": ")
        wr(2, &errbuf[0])
    wr(2, "\n")


def put_dec(fd: int32, v: uint64):
    if v == 0:
        sys_write(fd, "0", 1)
        return
    k: uint64 = 24
    while v > 0:
        k = k - 1
        numbuf[k] = cast[uint8](48 + cast[uint32](v % 10))
        v = v / 10
    sys_write(fd, &numbuf[k], 24 - k)


def now_ms() -> uint64:
    ts[0] = 0
    ts[1] = 0
    if sys_clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts[0]) != 0:
        return 0
    return ts[0] * 1000 + ts[1] / 1000000


def img_put(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    # Append a NUL-terminated string to the image, never past the end.
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and img_n < BOOTLOG_CAP:
        img[img_n] = s[i]
        img_n = img_n + 1
        i = i + 1


def build_image() -> int32:
    # WHAT THE 256 KiB FILE SHOULD LOOK LIKE, ENTIRELY IN MEMORY. Nothing here
    # touches the medium; §1 of the header is why that separation is the whole
    # design.
    rc: int32 = 0
    img_n = 0
    img_put("==== HAMNIX BOOT LOG ====\n")
    img_put("This is the kernel log ring of the boot that wrote it, including every\n")
    img_put("line the shell and the desktop printed (mirrored in as `cons:' lines).\n")
    img_put("Rewritten in place as the log grows; the tail is the newest.\n")
    img_put("-------------------------------------------------------------------\n")

    room: uint64 = BOOTLOG_CAP - img_n - TAIL_RESERVE
    n: int64 = sys_klogread(&img[img_n], room)
    if n < 0:
        # NAMED, NOT GUESSED AT. EPERM here means dmesg_restrict is set and we
        # are not root; an empty file with no explanation would be read as "the
        # boot printed nothing", which is the exact misreading this whole
        # arrangement exists to prevent.
        img_put("*** THE KERNEL LOG COULD NOT BE READ. This file is otherwise empty\n")
        img_put("*** for that reason and NOT because the boot printed nothing.\n")
        rc = -1
    else:
        img_n = img_n + cast[uint64](n)
    img_put("\n==== END OF HAMNIX BOOT LOG ====\n")

    # AND THE REST IS NEWLINES, WHICH IS THE WIPE, DONE IN MEMORY.
    #
    # It has to be part of the image rather than a one-off pass over the file:
    # what a PREVIOUS boot wrote past the end of what THIS boot writes is still
    # sitting on the medium, and somebody reading a short failed boot's log
    # would find this boot's few lines followed, with no seam, by the whole of
    # a boot that went fine. That is a worse failure than no log. Newlines
    # rather than NULs so the unused tail opens as ordinary empty text in any
    # editor rather than as a sea of NULs some of them refuse to display -- and
    # so that `img` contains no NUL, which is what makes the all-NUL `prev`
    # force a full write the first time round.
    while img_n < BOOTLOG_CAP:
        img[img_n] = 10                                 # '\n'
        img_n = img_n + 1
    return rc


def flush_changed() -> int64:
    # ONE lseek AND ONE write COVERING EXACTLY THE BYTES THAT DIFFER FROM WHAT
    # IS ON THE MEDIUM. Returns the number of bytes written -- 0 when there was
    # nothing to do, which on an idle machine is every time -- or -1.
    lo: uint64 = 0
    while lo < BOOTLOG_CAP and img[lo] == prev[lo]:
        lo = lo + 1
    if lo >= BOOTLOG_CAP:
        return 0
    hi: uint64 = BOOTLOG_CAP - 1
    while hi > lo and img[hi] == prev[hi]:
        hi = hi - 1
    if sys_lseek(logfd, cast[int64](lo), SEEK_SET) < 0:
        return -1
    w: int64 = sys_write(logfd, &img[lo], hi - lo + 1)
    if w <= 0:
        # The file is shorter than we thought, or the medium went away.
        # `prev` is left alone, so whatever did not land is simply still
        # pending and the next snapshot tries again from the same place.
        return -1
    # ONLY WHAT ACTUALLY LANDED IS RECORDED AS LANDED. A short write must not
    # let the tail it did not reach be treated as already on the stick.
    i: uint64 = lo
    e: uint64 = lo + cast[uint64](w)
    while i < e:
        prev[i] = img[i]
        i = i + 1
    return w


def snapshot() -> int32:
    rc: int32 = build_image()
    t0: uint64 = now_ms()
    w: int64 = flush_changed()
    took: uint64 = now_ms() - t0
    if w < 0:
        return -1

    # THE CADENCE FOLLOWS THE MEDIUM. A snapshot that wrote nothing says
    # nothing about how fast the stick is, so it does not move the interval in
    # either direction -- otherwise an idle machine would ratchet the cadence
    # down to SNAP_MS and hold it there on the strength of writes it never did.
    if w > 0:
        if took > SLOW_MS and interval < SNAP_MAX_MS:
            interval = interval * 2
            if interval > SNAP_MAX_MS:
                interval = SNAP_MAX_MS
            if backed_off == 0:
                backed_off = 1
                say("this boot medium is slow to write; snapshotting less often")
                say("so that logging cannot become the thing that stalls the machine.")
                say("a power cut now loses up to the interval below, in milliseconds:")
                wr(1, "bootlogd: ")
                put_dec(1, cast[uint64](interval))
                wr(1, "\n")
        elif took < FAST_MS and interval > SNAP_MS:
            interval = interval / 2
            if interval <= SNAP_MS:
                interval = SNAP_MS
                if backed_off != 0:
                    backed_off = 0
                    say("the boot medium is keeping up again; back to the 2 s cadence.")
    return rc


def main(argc: int32, argv: Ptr[uint64]) -> int32:
    once: int32 = 0
    i: int32 = 1
    while i < argc:
        a: Ptr[uint8] = cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[i])
        if a[0] == 45 and a[1] == 49 and a[2] == 0:     # "-1"
            once = 1
        i = i + 1

    logfd = sys_open_sync(cast[Ptr[char]]("/boot/HAMNIX.LOG"))
    if logfd < 0:
        # THE FAILURE PATH, AND IT IS A SUPPORTED OUTCOME. Say what is missing,
        # say what it costs, say what would fix it, and exit 0 so that nothing
        # anywhere reads this as a boot failure.
        sayerr("NO BOOT LOG: /boot/HAMNIX.LOG could not be opened for writing")
        say("the boot continues normally; only the on-stick log is unavailable.")
        say("expected a preallocated HAMNIX.LOG in the root of the FAT boot")
        say("partition, and `bind '#esp' /boot` to have mounted it there.")
        sys_exit(0)
        return 0

    say("logging this boot to HAMNIX.LOG in the root of the FAT boot partition")

    # THE FIRST SNAPSHOT IS THE FULL FILE, and it is that without a special
    # case: `prev` is all NULs and `img` contains none, so every byte differs.
    # That is also the wipe, in ONE write instead of the 32 the old startup
    # pass took.
    if snapshot() < 0:
        sayerr("the first snapshot failed; the log may be incomplete")
    if once != 0:
        sys_close(logfd)
        return 0

    # FOREVER, and deliberately not "until the boot is finished". There is no
    # such moment: the interesting failures are the ones where the desktop
    # comes up and then something goes wrong -- which is precisely what
    # happened on 2026-08-16, five minutes into a desktop that had come up
    # fine -- or where it never does and the machine sits there until he
    # reaches for the power button. Whenever he reaches for it, the file is at
    # most one interval behind the truth.
    #
    # IT COSTS NOTHING TO LEAVE RUNNING NOW. A pass over a ring that has not
    # changed is two scans of 256 KiB of memory and no transaction with the
    # medium at all.
    while True:
        sys_waitfds(&park_fds[0], 0, interval)
        snapshot()
    return 0
